        "original_id": "dfaa8e9d-11b4-58d7-b9e8-85796aa4c144",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Clement Greenberg, Agatha Christie, Joseph Drapell, Jack Bush\n- Organizations: Cranbrook Academy, Old Bailey\n- Works: Witness for the Prosecution, Witness for the Prosecution (1957 film)\n- Roles: art critic, essayist, crime novelist, short story writer, playwright, artist\n- Topics: American Modern art, murder trial\n\nFacts:\n- Agatha Christie was born on 15 September 1890 and became an English crime novelist, short story writer, and playwright.\n- Clement Greenberg was born on January 16, 1909, sometimes wrote under the pseudonym K. Hardesh, and became an American essayist and influential visual art critic associated with mid-20th-century American Modern art.\n- The 1957 film Witness for the Prosecution is set in the Old Bailey in London.\n- The 1957 film Witness for the Prosecution is based on Agatha Christie’s play of the same name and centers on the trial of a man accused of murder.\n- At Cranbrook Academy, Joseph Drapell met visiting Canadian artist Jack Bush and American art critic Clement Greenberg.\n- Clement Greenberg influenced Joseph Drapell’s work.\n- Agatha Christie died on 12 January 1976.\n- Clement Greenberg died on May 7, 1994.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
